      "time": 1789740928.126678,
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Done. `full_fil_data.csv` now has the `split` column: **23 train / 11 val** (32%).\n\n## Cleanup applied\n\nDropped the 7 blank `,,` rows your spreadsheet left behind, and restored `hemisphere`/`chirality` to `int64` \u2014 the NaN rows had silently promoted both to `float64`, which would have written `1.0` back out.\n\n## Split\n\nStratified on hemisphere \u00d7 chirality, seeded (`SEED = 42`):\n\n```\n                   chirality 0   chirality 1\ntrain  hem 0            6             3\n       hem 1            6             8\nval    hem 0            2             2\n       hem 1            3             4\n```\n\nMartin's Rule: 0.609 train / 0.545 val / 0.588 overall. Your manual removals shifted this off the artificial 50/50 the earlier version had, which is a more honest baseline.\n\n**Verified:** 34 rows, no NaN anywhere, no duplicate timestamps, no train/val overlap, no same-label pair within 3 days (closest is 4d22h \u2014 `2011-06-02`/`2011-06-07`, both in train). Both splits resolve fully against `surya_aws_s3_full_index.csv` (23\u219223, 11\u219211, nothing lost to the `2d` tolerance) and all 34 S3 objects exist in `nasa-surya-bench`.\n\n## Script changed role\n\n`make_splits.py` no longer removes anything \u2014 since you own dedup now, it *checks* the property instead and **raises** with the offending pairs if a \u22643-day same-label pair reappears, rather than quietly splitting it. It also drops blank rows and rejects partially-empty ones. Re-running it only rewrites the `split` column; rows and labels are untouched.\n\n## Two loose ends\n\n**Stale file.** `full_fil_data_dedup.csv` from the previous turn is now superseded and carries a *different* split for a 35-row catalog. Want me to delete it? Nothing references it \u2014 the config still points at `basic_fil_data.csv` (`configs/config_script.yaml:60`), so switch that to `../data/full_fil_data.csv` when you're ready.\n\n**One physically interesting straddle.** `2012-06-14` (train) and `2012-07-12` (val) are 28d02h apart, same hemisphere and chirality. That's within a few hours of one solar rotation (27.3d), so it's plausibly the *same* filament returning on the next passage rather than a new one. Well outside your 3-day rule, so I left it \u2014 but it's the one remaining pair I'd want a second look at, since a rotation-recurrent filament in both splits leaks the same way a same-day duplicate does.",
